# Drafting timesheet customer notes

Files in `exports/` are generated by the overlay app. Each has a **Timesheet lines**
table (the hours to key in) and a **Work carried out, by project** section (the raw
material for the narrative).

## Output shape

Produce one block per K-code, ready to paste:

```
K-10234 — Acme Ltd / Platform Migration — 18.25h
Completed the data migration runbook and walked the client's platform team through
the cutover sequence. Resolved three failing integration tests blocking the staging
rehearsal, and confirmed the rollback path with their DBA.
```

## Rules

- **Audience is the customer**, not your manager. Past tense, plain English, no jargon
  the client wouldn't use themselves.
- **1–3 sentences per K-code.** Aggregate the week; don't narrate day by day unless the
  dates matter to the client.
- **Lead with the outcome**, then the supporting work. "Delivered X" beats "worked on X".
- **Use the hours from the Timesheet lines table verbatim.** Never recompute or round them.
- **Internal NCC clients get a shorter, internal-voice note** — no customer framing.
- **Drop internal chatter**: blockers you caused, tooling grumbles, anything in Day notes
  that the client shouldn't read. Day notes are context for you, not copy for them.
- **Never invent work.** If a line has no notes attached, say so and ask rather than
  filling the gap with plausible-sounding detail.

## Billable vs non-billable

Billable lines get the full narrative. Non-billable lines can be one short clause
(`Internal project admin and timesheet upkeep.`) unless the export says otherwise.
